@file sybase-cdc-connector/src/main/java/com/phoenix/cdc/sybase/cdc/AckTracker.java
@summary
<p><b>Role:</b> converts Kafka's per-record acknowledgements into one safe log position, the <em>watermark</em>. The watermark is the commit OQID of the newest transaction such that it <em>and every transaction before it</em> is completely in Kafka. RepAgent receives this value in <code>get truncation</code>, so ASE only frees log space that Kafka holds. See diagram <a href="../index.html#fig-17">17</a>.</p>
<p><b>Threads:</b> <code>register()</code> runs on the RepAgent session thread; <code>ack()</code> runs on the Kafka producer's callback thread; <code>acked()</code> runs on the session thread. All public methods are <code>synchronized</code>. <b>Tests:</b> <code>AckTrackerTest</code> (out-of-order acks, empty transaction). <b>End-to-end evidence:</b> test P5 (Kafka paused: watermark frozen, then advanced).</p>
@@ 1
<p>Package <code>cdc</code>.</p>
@@ 3-4
<p><code>Deque</code> ("double-ended queue") interface and its array-backed implementation <code>ArrayDeque</code>: add at the end, look at and remove from the front in O(1) time.</p>
@@ 6-15
<p>Class comment stating the invariant. The key fact: a single transaction's records may go to different topics (one per table), and Kafka can confirm topics independently, so acknowledgements can arrive out of order.</p>
@@ 16
<p>Final class; one instance per task.</p>
@@ 18-26
<p><b>Nested static class <code>Pending</code>:</b> one per transaction that was registered: its commit OQID and how many of its records are still unacknowledged. <code>static</code> means it does not hold a reference to the outer tracker. Fields are package-private (no modifier), so only code in package <code>cdc</code> can read them; the task only passes the object back. <code>remaining</code> is mutated only inside the tracker's synchronized methods, which is what makes it safe without being <code>volatile</code>.</p>
@@ 28-29
<p>The ordered queue of transactions not yet fully acknowledged, in commit order, and the current watermark. Both are guarded by the object's lock (every method that touches them is <code>synchronized</code>).</p>
@@ 31-33
<p>Constructor. <code>initial</code> is the commit OQID read from Kafka Connect's stored offset at task start (or <code>null</code> on the very first start). Starting from the stored offset means the first <code>get truncation</code> after a restart returns a position Kafka already holds, rather than zeros.</p>
@@ 35-41
<p><b>Register a transaction.</b> Called in commit order, once per committed transaction that will be published, with the number of Kafka records it produces. A transaction with 0 records (e.g. it touched only tables excluded by <code>table.include.list</code>, or only DDL markers) is registered with 0 so that it can move the watermark too; otherwise ASE could never truncate past a long stretch of such transactions. <code>advance()</code> is called immediately because a 0-record transaction may already be complete. Returns the <code>Pending</code> so each Kafka record can carry a reference to it.</p>
@@ 43-47
<p><b>One record confirmed.</b> Called from <code>SybaseCdcSourceTask.commitRecord()</code>, which Kafka Connect invokes after the producer receives the broker's acknowledgement. Decrements the counter and tries to move the watermark. <b>Note:</b> Kafka Connect also calls <code>commitRecord</code> with <code>metadata == null</code> for records dropped by a transform or by <code>errors.tolerance=all</code>; such records would count as acknowledged. The deployment therefore keeps <code>errors.tolerance=none</code> (documented in the configuration chapter).</p>
@@ 49-52
<p>Returns the current watermark (or <code>null</code>). <code>Oqid.locator()</code> turns <code>null</code> into 36 zero bytes for RepAgent.</p>
@@ 54-56
<p>Number of transactions still waiting for acknowledgements. <b>Currently unused</b> by production code; kept as a natural metric to expose in the future (see the monitoring follow-up).</p>
@@ 58-61
<p>Clears in-flight transactions. <b>Currently unused:</b> the task does not call it, because a RepAgent reconnect does not invalidate records already handed to Kafka; they will still be acknowledged. Documented here so nobody assumes it runs.</p>
@@ 63-67
<p><b>The watermark rule.</b> While the oldest pending transaction has no unacknowledged records left, remove it and make its OQID the new watermark. The loop stops at the first transaction that is still waiting, even if later ones are complete. This is exactly the property that makes truncation safe. <b>If changed</b> (e.g. taking the maximum acknowledged OQID): a crash after T2 was acknowledged but before T1 was would release T1's log records and lose T1.</p>
@@ 68
<p>End of class.</p>
